{
  // Type-checks the TEST files, which `tsconfig.json` deliberately excludes from the BUILD (job-queue-014).
  //
  // Nothing ran this config until now, and the cost was not hypothetical: exhaustive
  // `{ [N in JobName]: ... }` maps in the queue tests had silently stopped being exhaustive — one was
  // missing four of twelve job names, another was missing `dub` — while the suite stayed green, because
  // vitest executes TypeScript without checking it. An exhaustive map that is not exhaustive is
  // worse than no map: it reads as a guarantee.
  //
  // `src/_archive/**` is excluded for the same reason the build excludes it — it is retired code kept
  // for reference, its imports point at modules that have since moved, and type-checking it would
  // bury the 4 real errors under 240 dead ones. Overriding `exclude` without carrying that entry
  // forward is what made this config unusable even for anyone who tried to run it.
  // `lib` adds DOM **here and only here**. `tsconfig.base.json` sets `lib: ["ES2022"]` with no DOM,
  // deliberately: this is a Node server, and a production file that reaches for `document` or
  // `window` should fail to compile rather than fail at runtime. That guarantee is unchanged —
  // `tsconfig.json` still has no DOM, so nothing under `src/` outside a test can see those globals.
  //
  // The tests need them because some of this package's output IS browser code: the rAF gate, the
  // combined bridge and the v3 child runtime are all authored as ES5 strings and shipped into a
  // document. Until now the only way to test them was to assert on their source text, which is how
  // a defective selector line ended up pinned as if it were the specification. Running them in
  // jsdom means the test file legitimately handles `Window`, `Document` and `HTMLInputElement`.
  //
  // Declared EXPLICITLY even though it is redundant: `@types/jsdom/index.d.ts` opens with
  // `/// <reference lib="dom" />`, so any file importing jsdom pulls lib.dom into the whole program
  // whatever this says. Stating it here means the next reader finds the reason in the config rather
  // than inferring it from a transitive reference — and it is why four `new Blob([buffer])` call
  // sites in production now pass `new Uint8Array(buffer)`: `Buffer<ArrayBufferLike>` does not
  // satisfy DOM's `BlobPart`, and that resolution changes for `src/**` the moment DOM is present.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"],
    "types": ["vitest/globals"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "src/_archive/**"]
}
